# Response headers, served by the host.
#
# These were in the Go middleware, and before that in an nginx.conf that was
# never part of the running topology — so the policy below was advertised for
# months while no response actually carried it. Keeping them here means they are
# in the repository, in review, and verifiable: the contract test asserts the
# values a real request comes back with, not that a file exists.
#
# The rules must not overlap. Cloudflare merges every matching rule instead of
# letting the most specific one win, so a font matching both /static/* and
# /static/fonts/* comes back with `max-age=3600, max-age=31536000` — two
# directives in one header, and the browser takes the first. Measured, not
# assumed: the first version of this file did exactly that.

# script-src carries static.cloudflareinsights.com because Cloudflare injects its
# Web Analytics beacon into HTML at the edge — enabled by default for proxied
# zones, present in no file this repository builds. Until 2026-07-30 the policy
# refused it on every page, so the analytics collected nothing while each visit
# still paid for a blocked request.
#
# The origin is allowed rather than the path the docs suggest. The injected URL
# is .../beacon.min.js/v4513226cd..., and a CSP source whose path does not end in
# a slash must match the path exactly — so `.../beacon.min.js` would match
# nothing. That host serves this one script, so restricting the path buys nothing
# real and adds a way for a version bump to break it silently.
#
# connect-src needs no change: under automatic injection the beacon reports to
# /cdn-cgi/rum on this origin, which 'self' already covers. Verified that
# /cdn-cgi/* is answered by Cloudflare ahead of the Worker rather than falling
# through to 404.html.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=()
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://cdn.jsdelivr.net https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'

# Stylesheet and scripts are busted by a ?v= query that nothing enforces the
# incrementing of. An hour plus revalidation drops nearly every round trip
# without betting on anyone remembering; immutable would strand a returning
# visitor on a stale stylesheet with no way to recover.
/static/css/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/static/js/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

# Images change when their content does, and their names change with them.
/static/og/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/static/brand/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

# Listed one by one rather than as /static/*.png, because Cloudflare's `*`
# crosses directory separators: that glob also matched /static/og/index.png,
# which then came back carrying two identical max-age directives.
/static/android-chrome-192x192.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/static/android-chrome-512x512.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/static/apple-touch-icon.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/static/favicon-16x16.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/static/favicon-32x32.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/static/favicon.ico
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/favicon.ico
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/site.webmanifest
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

# No font rule, because there are no font files. The self-hosted faces went
# with the last two brand resets — the mono pair first, then D-DIN — and the
# stacks in tailwind.config.js now start at SF Pro and fall through to the
# reader's own CJK face (decision #54). The rule for /static/fonts/* outlived
# the directory by two resets: it matched nothing, cached nothing, and read
# like evidence that the site still ships a typeface.

# THE MARKDOWN TWINS POINT BACK AT THE HTML THEY WERE MADE FROM (issue #258).
#
# `/zh-Hant-TW/geo-guide.md` and `/zh-Hant-TW/geo-guide` are the same content at
# two URLs, which is duplicate content. Markdown has no `<head>`, so it cannot
# carry a `<link rel="canonical">`; the HTTP `Link` header is the mechanism for
# exactly that case and is the one Google documents for non-HTML files.
#
# TWO RULES BECAUSE A LOCALE HOME IS ONE SEGMENT AND A PAGE IS TWO. Measured
# against `wrangler dev` rather than assumed, which is the only reason this
# file has the second rule: `/:locale/:page.md` matched
# `/zh-Hant-TW/geo-guide.md` and returned `slug=geo-guide` — a placeholder does
# stop before a literal suffix — and matched `/zh-Hant-TW.md` not at all. The
# first version of this block had one rule and would have shipped five locale
# homes with no canonical and nothing saying so.
#
# THEY DO NOT OVERLAP WITH EACH OTHER, which matters more here than usual: the
# comment at the top of this file records that Cloudflare MERGES every matching
# rule instead of letting the most specific win, so two rules both setting
# `Link` would produce one header carrying two canonicals — and a parser takes
# the first. Verified by fetching both shapes and counting the header.
#
# They do overlap `/*`, which is fine and unavoidable: `/*` sets no `Link`, so
# the merge is of distinct header names rather than of two values for one.
#
# No Cache-Control here on purpose. `.md` follows HTML's default; adding a tier
# is the overlap hazard above for no measured benefit.
#
# ⚠️ THE CONTENT-TYPE IS SET HERE BECAUSE PRODUCTION DROPS THE CHARSET, AND THE
# ORIGINAL MEASUREMENT MISSED IT.
#
# When the twins landed, `wrangler dev` was measured answering
# `text/markdown; charset=utf-8` and a browser was measured rendering them
# inline. Both were true of the emulator and neither survived deployment:
# production answers `text/markdown` with no charset at all — it does the same
# to HTML (`text/html`, bare) and nobody noticed, because an HTML document
# carries `<meta charset="UTF-8">` and rescues itself.
#
# MARKDOWN HAS NO SUCH IN-BAND DECLARATION. Measured against the deployed site:
# Chromium decoded every `.md` as Latin-1, so `GEO 生成式引擎優化完整指南` was
# served to a reader as `GEO ç”Ÿæˆå¼å¼•æ“Žå„ªåŒ–å®Œæ•´æŒ‡å—`. Four locales out of
# five are CJK; the whole class of file was mojibake in a browser from the hour
# it shipped.
#
# NOTES.md already names this category — the defects only "a browser plus
# production" can see. The contract audit HAD the assertion that catches it,
# comparing the Content-Type exactly rather than for presence; it runs against
# the emulator, where the header was already right. The assertion was never
# wrong. The environment it ran in could not see the difference.
#
# So: run `BASE_URL=https://taux.io npm run contract` after a deploy that
# touches this block. That is the only place the value is real.
#
# Verified locally that this REPLACES rather than merges — one `Content-Type`
# header comes back, not two, which is the hazard the top of this file opens
# by naming. Production has to be re-measured after this deploys; the local
# result cannot prove it, because locally the emulator was already sending the
# value this line asks for.

/:locale/:page.md
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8
  Link: <https://taux.io/:locale/:page>; rel="canonical"

/:home.md
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8
  Link: <https://taux.io/:home>; rel="canonical"

# THE SAME MISSING CHARSET, ONE FILE LATER, AND IT WAS SHIPPING TOO.
#
# The block above was written for `.md` and stopped there. `llms.txt` is served
# `text/plain` with no charset by the same production layer that strips it from
# everything else — so the file this site's whole GEO argument is aimed at read
# `# TauX æ‹“æ€ç§‘æŠ€` in a browser from the day it was written. Measured
# against the deployed site, not guessed.
#
# THE COUNT, WITH THE RANGES NAMED SO THERE IS ONLY ONE WAY TO DO IT. Of 208
# lines, 142 carry non-ASCII: 114 carry Han, 132 counting Hangul as well, and
# the remaining ten are English lines holding a `→` or a `—`.
#
# ⚠️ THIS SAID "142 OF ITS 208 LINES ARE CHINESE", which is the non-ASCII count
# wearing the wrong name. The paragraph below congratulates itself for catching
# exactly that conflation in robots.txt, three lines after committing it here,
# and both were labelled "measured". Two reviewers then produced two different
# CJK figures from two different range sets, which is why the ranges are spelt
# out above rather than left to whoever counts next.
#
# ROBOTS.TXT IS IN HERE TOO, and the first read of this was that it did not need
# to be. It carries seven non-ASCII lines — `→`, `§`, `—` in the comments — so
# "it is pure ASCII" was an assumption, and counting took one command. The harm
# there is only cosmetic, since no crawler reads the prose, but there is no
# version of "serve half the text files with the wrong charset" that is worth
# defending.
#
# ONE GLOB RATHER THAN FOUR RULES, and that is measured. Cloudflare's `*`
# crosses directory separators — the comment above the icon rules says so, where
# it is a hazard — so `/*.txt` reaches `/static/llms.txt` as readily as
# `/llms.txt`. Verified against `wrangler dev`: all four files, one
# `Content-Type` header each, and `.md` and HTML untouched.

# WHERE THE GUARD IS, because it is not where you would look. `contract`
# asserts these four files, but only in its `AGAINST_ORIGIN` branch: measured,
# `wrangler dev` supplies the charset by itself, so an assertion that ran
# locally would pass whether or not this rule existed. Run
# `BASE_URL=https://taux.io npm run contract` after a deploy that touches this
# block; `zone.yml` also runs it daily.

/*.txt
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
